#
# The trust store for this project's release tags: the binding from a public
# key to the name it signs under.  Without it `git verify-tag` has a valid
# signature and no idea whose it should be, and cannot report anything.
#
# Verify a release tag against it:
#
#     git -c gpg.ssh.allowedSignersFile=.github/allowed_signers verify-tag v5.0.0
#
# The key below is the Ed25519 key registered on the maintainer's GitHub
# account as a *signing* key, which is why signed tags render "Verified" on
# github.com.  It is the same key that signed v4.0.0, and
# tests/test_release_tag_trust_store.py checks that on every CI run by
# verifying the v4.0.0 tag object against this file — so this entry is
# evidence rather than an assertion.
#
#     fingerprint  SHA256:1MSkOHmeGP16tdSg705wY6rwFm+odfU3cUo0UwlfAP4
#
# Scoped to namespaces="git": the key is trusted for git object signatures and
# nothing else.  Format: ssh-keygen(1), ALLOWED SIGNERS.
steel.sa.llc@gmail.com namespaces="git" ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAINkxen8sxqRE6kq0ZIj3PcnB/WbBUqpJMmg+pdWXTxlD
